.page ping 1 send ICMP echo requests to a host

# SYNOPSIS
*ping* [*-4* | *-6*] [*-c* _COUNT_] [*-i* _SECONDS_] [*-W* _SECONDS_] [*-s* _BYTES_] [*-t* _TTL_] [*-q*] _HOST_

# DESCRIPTION
`ping` sends an ICMP echo request to _HOST_ (a name, a dotted address or an IPv6 address) once a second and prints a line for every answer: the size of the reply, the sender, the sequence number, the time to live that was left in it, and the round-trip time. Without `-c` it goes on until interrupted with Ctrl+C. At the end it prints how many packets were sent and received and the shortest, average and longest round trip.

It uses an ICMP datagram socket, so any user may run it. Times are measured with the one-millisecond clock of the kernel: a reply on the same machine shows as 0 or 1 ms.

If a router or the stack itself answers with an error, the line says so (`Destination Host Unreachable`, `Time to live exceeded`) instead of a reply. A request that gets no answer within the timeout prints `Request timeout for icmp_seq N`.

**IPv4 or IPv6.** A name is looked up for an IPv4 address first; a name that has none, and an address with a colon in it (`::1`, `fec0::2`, `fe80::2%eth0`), is pinged over IPv6 (ICMPv6 echo). `-4` and `-6` force one family; `ping -6` is the command `ping6(1)`. An IPv6 line shows no time to live (the socket does not hand over the hop limit): `64 bytes from fec0::2: icmp_seq=1 time=1 ms`. A link-local address needs the interface: `fe80::2%eth0` (without it, `ping` uses the only card there is; with several cards the request fails with `Invalid argument`).

# OPTIONS
@ *-4*, *-6*
    Use IPv4 or IPv6 only (the name is looked up for that family).
@ *-c* _COUNT_
    Stop after COUNT requests.
@ *-i* _SECONDS_
    Wait this long between requests (at least 0.2, default 1).
@ *-W* _SECONDS_
    Wait this long for each answer (default 2).
@ *-s* _BYTES_
    Size of the data in the request (8 to 1400, default 56; over IPv6 up to 65000, bigger requests are sent as fragments).
@ *-t* _TTL_
    Time to live (IPv6: hop limit) of the outgoing packets.
@ *-q*
    Quiet: only the summary.

# EXAMPLES
    ping -c 3 10.0.2.2
    ping -c 1 localhost
    ping -6 -c 2 fec0::2
    ping -c 1 ::1

# EXIT STATUS
0 if at least one answer arrived, 1 if none did, 2 for a usage error or a name that does not resolve.

# SEE ALSO
ping6(1), nslookup(1), ifconfig(1), network(7)
